    "created_at": "2026-09-10T18:07:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-10T18:07:36Z",
    "body": "> * Do we need `<style type=module>` as well?\r\n\r\n@LeaVerou - this was discussed a few weeks ago at WHATNOT and added to the [design doc](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SkHwxAIBW5I3uqnmmov4D71ZPbj9woouj3RdPqd3X1w/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.km669jy66bmr). More details in [this comment in the old PR](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12339#issuecomment-5455453483). Please add more pros and cons to the design doc if you think of any that are missing.\r\n\r\nThis PR is no longer using `type=module`, see [this comment in the old PR](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12339#issuecomment-5451014413) for more details.\r\n\r\n> * There is nothing on how `@import` behaves in a module context. I'd imagine `type=module` should affect that (these should become part of the module graph and be fetched accordingly, just like `<link type=module>`).\r\n\r\nOne of the main goals of this proposal is parity with importing a CSS module via script. Any changes to the module graph would require changes to how `@import` works in existing CSS module script imports. So I don't believe any changes are necessary in this PR for CSS `@import`, but any future changes to `@import` in a module context would also apply here.\r\n\r\n> It would also be good to file for a TAG review on this, as it's a significant architectural change.\r\n\r\nThe existing [existing TAG response](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1195#issuecomment-4608033622) also supported using `<link>` for this scenario.",
