    "body": "> @LeaVerou - you're right, `nomodule` is a possibility here, and it's not covered in the explainer. With fallback, the two options would look like:\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> <link rel=stylesheet href=./styles/foo.css import=foo>\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> vs\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> <link rel=stylesheet type=module href=foo>\r\n> <link rel=stylesheet href=./styles/foo.css nomodule>\r\n\r\nI think either is fine. Let's pick one and do this.\r\n\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> But we also have `<link rel=modulepreload>` that uses `href` [that adds an entry to the module map](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#link-type-modulepreload) and does not require `type=module`. So it really comes down to consistency between different types of`<link>`s vs consistency between `<link>` and `<script>`.\r\n\r\nI wouldn't worry too much about consistency with `modulepreload`. It's a performance optimization and it's OK that it's a URL and not a specifier.",
